RAWRXD_RESIDUAL_OVERLAY_SEGMENT_001
=====================================
THE OVERLAY'S OWN FIDELITY/BITRATE SEGMENT, AND AN ALGEBRAIC BOUND
THAT CLOSES THE LOW-RANK VARIANT PROVABLY.

TOOL   rawrxd/certs/gguf_tensor_slice_001.cpp
MODELS dense blk31.attn_q(Q4_K) blk63.ffn_down(Q6_K)
       MoE   blk20.ffn_down_exps(IQ3_XXS) blk0.ffn_gate_exps(IQ2_XS)
WINDOW 256 x 1024     ALL CASES PASS, CHECKS_FAIL=0


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THE SEGMENT -- WHAT THE OVERLAY ACTUALLY BUYS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
tensor                        base fmt  base bpw  base-only rel_L2
                                            |  +delta bpw  full rel_L2
------------------------------------------------------------
blk.31.attn_q.weight           Q4_K       4.5000    0.814574   11.711  0.000000
blk.63.ffn_down.weight         Q6_K       6.5625    0.763574   13.419  0.000000
blk.20.ffn_down_exps.weight    IQ3_XXS    3.0625    0.690447    7.299  0.000000
blk.0.ffn_gate_exps.weight     IQ2_XS     2.3125    0.664453    7.257  0.000000

Each row is the overlay's own Pareto segment: point A is the quantised base
alone, point B is base plus delta and is EXACT BY CONSTRUCTION (the delta is
defined as W - base, so point B has zero error and tells us nothing about
quality -- only what it costs).

THE OVERLAY DOES COMPRESS. For MoE experts it reaches exact reconstruction at
7.26-7.30 bits/element against a 32-bit fp16 original -- a 4.4x reduction. That
is a real result and it is not nothing.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. CORRECTION TO MY OWN PRIOR VERDICT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The previous receipt concluded:

    RESIDUAL_OVERLAY_COMPRESSION = NOT VIABLE on every reachable class

That was stated as though it covered the whole proposal. IT DOES NOT. Every one
of those 21 cases tested ONE variant -- the LOW-RANK residual. That variant is
dead (section 3). The general overlay is not dead: the segment above shows it
achieves 4.4x on MoE experts.

The honest position is narrower:

    LOW_RANK_RESIDUAL   = REFUTED, provably (section 3)
    OVERLAY_GENERAL     = COMPRESSES 4.4x, but UNTESTED against the right rival

A reader who took the earlier verdict as "the idea is dead" would have been
misled, and the fault is mine for the wording rather than the measurement.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. WHY THE LOW-RANK VARIANT IS PROVABLY DEAD -- AND THE VICTORY BELONGS TO ALGEBRA
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
If W = B + D then, by the subadditivity of rank,

        rank(W)  <=  rank(B) + rank(D)

So a LOW-RANK residual requires a HIGH-RANK base. And factor cost is
proportional to rank, so for an R x C matrix:

        two_factor_cost  =  (rank(B) + rank(D)) * (R + C) * 32 / (R * C)
                         >=  rank(W) * per_rank_cost

MEASURED rank(W) itself, same exact Jacobi spectrum:

  dense Q4_K  blk.31.attn_q      rank(W)@99% = 236 / 256  (92.2% full rank)
                                 MIN_TWO_FACTOR_COST = 36.875 bits/elem
  dense Q6_K  blk.63.ffn_down    rank(W)@99% = 247 / 256  (96.5% full rank)
                                 MIN_TWO_FACTOR_COST = 38.594 bits/elem
  MoE IQ3_XXS blk.20.ffn_down_exps 247 / 256              38.594 bits/elem
  MoE IQ2_XS  blk.0.ffn_gate_exps  247 / 256              38.594 bits/elem

THE MINIMUM COST OF ANY TWO-FACTOR DECOMPOSITION EXCEEDS THE 32-BIT ORIGINAL.

This is not an empirical loss of a race; it is a floor. W is 92-96% full rank,
so no split into two factors can be cheaper than storing W. No cleverness in the
factorisation changes it, and no better quantiser changes it. The only way out
is for rank(W) to be small, and these tensors are not.

This is why the earlier 0/21 on low-rank was not bad luck. It was arithmetic.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. THE ONE COMPARISON THAT WAS NEVER MADE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The overlay's rival is NOT fp32. It is the best SINGLE-FORMAT quantiser at the
same total bitrate.

For the MoE experts, point B costs 7.26-7.30 bpw for exact reconstruction.
Single formats in that neighbourhood are already shipped and need no second
decode pass:

    IQ2_XS   2.3125 bpw        Q4_K   4.5000 bpw
    IQ3_XXS  3.0625 bpw        Q5_K   5.5000 bpw
                              Q6_K   6.5625 bpw
                              Q8_0   8.5000 bpw

So the decisive question is:

    At ~7.3 bpw total, does (base + delta) reconstruct W better than a single
    7.3-bpw format reconstructs it?

Point B has rel_L2 = 0.000000 BY CONSTRUCTION, so the overlay's advantage is
that it is EXACT. The rival's error at the same bitrate is unmeasured.

If a single format reaches rel_L2 < ~0.05 at 7.3 bpw, the overlay is dominated
-- it costs the same, needs a second decode pass, a second memory tier, and a
fetch for the delta, and returns an error that the single format nearly matches.
If no single format does, the overlay wins on fidelity per bit and the
architecture is justified.

THIS MEASUREMENT HAS NOT BEEN RUN. It is cheap: the dequant machinery already
exists for every one of these formats, and the harness already computes rel_L2
against a fixed probe vector. What is missing is a direct re-quantisation sweep
of W at each bpw.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. WHAT IS SETTLED AND WHAT IS NOT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
SETTLED, provably:
    LOW-RANK RESIDUAL      DEAD. The rank bound puts the floor at 36.9-38.6
                           bits/element for a 32-bit original. Not a tuning
                           problem.
    ORDER-0 CODING OF THE DELTA  Wins against low rank everywhere (0/21), but
                           it costs 4.2-7.2 bits/element ON TOP of the base,
                           which is why point B lands at 7.3-13.4 bpw.

SETTLED, measured:
    The overlay compresses 32 -> 7.3 bpw on MoE experts (4.4x), exact.
    MoE deltas are cheaper than dense (3.5-4.9 vs 6.9-7.2 b/e) but their
    spectra are nearly flat (sigma_1/sigma_R 2.99-3.41 vs 482-3146 dense).
    The delta is neither sparse, nor low-rank, nor low-entropy.

NOT SETTLED, and it is the only thing left:
    Overlay vs best single format at equal bitrate. Unmeasured.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. THE HONEST SUMMARY
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The proposal arrived as "split the weights so the correction field becomes
cheap." Measurement says the correction field is not cheap in ANY available
sense -- not sparse, not low-rank, not low-entropy -- and that the low-rank
framing is not merely ineffective but arithmetically excluded for tensors this
dense.

What survives is narrower and more interesting: a quantised base plus an
order-0-coded exact residual, costing 7.3 bpw on real MoE experts, with the
delta fetched separately and applied in a second pass. That is a real 4.4x, and
it has a real cost -- a second tier of storage, a second decode, and a fetch
whose latency is paid on the hot path.

Whether that trade is worth making is a measurement, not an argument, and it is
the next one. It is small.

VERDICT
  LOW_RANK_RESIDUAL_VARIANT   = REFUTED PROVABLY (rank bound > 32 bits)
  RESIDUAL_OVERLAY_GENERAL     = COMPRESSES 4.4x, RIVAL NOT YET MEASURED
  DELTA_STRUCTURE              = NONE FOUND (sparse / low-rank / low-entropy all fail)